{"state": "Morrow Books \u2014 fulfillment-control extract, revision 6. Section 2 assigns exception levels: 0 means no operative exception; 1 means requested but unauthorized; 2 means authorized but unused; 3 means used for a shipment. Section 4 says an authorization may be withdrawn until the warehouse first relies on it by bypassing validation or releasing the parcel. Section 7 says packing notes are evidentiary only: copying an authorization does not itself constitute reliance, and a note copied after withdrawal does not restore authority. A portal address correction processed normally is not use of an exception.\n\nCase 44: On 3 May a supervisor authorized one address override. At 08:15 on 4 May the customer corrected the address through the ordinary portal. At 08:22 the supervisor wrote, \u201cWithdraw the override; standard address validation now applies.\u201d At 08:31 a clerk, working from yesterday's queue, copied the authorization into the packing note and marked \u201coverride ready.\u201d The validation log at 08:44 shows the corrected address passed standard validation; bypass_used=false. The parcel remains in the cage. A shift summary calls the order \u201cauthorized for override,\u201d but it was generated from packing-note text rather than the authority register.", "model": "ruvector-native-submission-round2-v2", "questions": {"decision": {"type": "score", "instructions": "Assign the current fulfillment-exception level.", "criteria": ["No operative exception.", "Requested but unauthorized exception.", "Authorized, unused exception.", "Authorized exception already used."]}}}